WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.496
A process environment usually comes from whatever launched the program.

2
00:00:03.496 --> 00:00:12.237
That might be an interactive shell, a service manager, a container runtime, a CI runner, a scheduled task, an installer, a parent process, or a

3
00:00:12.237 --> 00:00:12.936
deployment platform.

4
00:00:12.936 --> 00:00:19.229
The trust level of each value depends on who controls that launch path and how changes are approved.

5
00:00:19.229 --> 00:00:25.872
This matters because environment variables can look like internal configuration even when they are supplied from outside the application.

6
00:00:25.872 --> 00:00:32.515
A value injected by a protected deployment system may deserve different trust than one inherited from an interactive shell.

7
00:00:32.515 --> 00:00:39.157
A service account may receive a carefully curated environment, while a local developer run may include many unrelated values.

8
00:00:39.157 --> 00:00:40.905
Child processes add another boundary.

9
00:00:40.905 --> 00:00:46.150
Unless the program constructs a controlled environment, a child may inherit values from its parent.

10
00:00:46.150 --> 00:00:53.841
That can accidentally pass configuration, secrets, search paths, debug flags, or library options to tools that were never meant to receive them.

11
00:00:53.841 --> 00:00:57.687
Inheritance should be a deliberate choice rather than a default assumption.

12
00:00:57.687 --> 00:01:05.029
Security-sensitive applications should document which environment variables are allowed, where they are expected to come from, and which sources are

13
00:01:05.029 --> 00:01:06.777
trusted enough to affect behavior.

14
00:01:06.777 --> 00:01:14.119
That documentation helps code review, operations, and incident response because the team can reason about environment influence as a visible contract.

15
00:01:14.119 --> 00:01:22.860
A useful review question is, "who could set this value before the process starts?" The answer may be a developer, a service operator, a container

16
00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:25.307
manifest, a CI job, or another executable.

17
00:01:25.307 --> 00:01:31.600
Once the owner is clear, the team can decide whether the value needs stricter validation, approval, or sanitization.
